{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_550c40c7471f49768e1d4e9c903e3e74","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The bug: the docstring says \"replace consecutive non-alphanumerics with a single '-', and strip leading/trailing '-'\", but the implementation is:\n1. Lowercasing — no problem.\n2. `re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]\", \"-\", text)` — replaces each non-alphanumeric character with one dash (doesn't collapse consecutive characters).\n3. Leading/trailing dashes are not being stripped.\n\nFix: either use `re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]+\", \"-\", text).strip(\"-\")`, or use `re.sub(r\"[-]+\", \"-\", re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]+\", \"-\", text))`.\n\nThe simplest form:\n```python\ntext = re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]+\", \"-\", text)\nreturn text.strip(\"-\")\n```\n\nVerifying: `\"  Hello,   World!  \"`.lower() → `\"  hello,   world!  \"`. re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]+\", \"-\", ...) → \"-hello-world-\". strip(\"-\") → \"hello-world\".\n\nAlso, should we keep the two regex steps? The `+` quantifier handles both collapsing consecutive characters and replacement in a single pass. Let's apply the fix.\n","signature":""}],"model":"Qwen3.8-27B-FP8","usage":{"input_tokens":16448,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49800677-a621-48af-822f-d4aa45eafb15","uuid":"da7350fb-042b-4d18-a086-bca82f480b81","timestamp":"2026-09-30T19:13:36.021Z"}
